# Edit Modbus read ranges in their own module settings dialog

Spec rev 6 · 4 stages · 15 entries (15 new, 0 changed) · coverage 93.14% · review clean

## Why
Modbus read ranges are edited in a separate module settings dialog (`:module`), so loading a module from a device-config file keeps that file's ranges.
Today confirming the setup dialog to load a file applies the dialog's range inputs over the file's ranges.

## Changes
- ferrowl `module::modbus::settings_dialog` — new module settings dialog: four range inputs prefilled from the device config, error border per input, live error box, `Enter` refused while an input fails, `Esc` through the close-confirmation popup (MB-R-288, MB-R-289, MB-R-291, MB-R-292, MB-R-294, MB-R-301)
- ferrowl `module::modbus::view` — `:module` command on client and server tabs, confirm adopts the ranges in memory and in the running module, writes no file (MB-R-288, MB-R-293, MB-R-296, MB-R-297)
- ferrowl `module::modbus::monitor::view` — `:module` left unhandled on monitor tabs (MB-R-290)
- ferrowl `module::modbus::module` — read ranges replaced at runtime, poll operations rebuilt in place without reconnecting, gap cells of the new ranges declared (MB-R-295, MB-R-296, MB-E-105)
- ferrowl `module::modbus::config::device` — strict range check shared by the dialog (MB-R-291)
- ferrowl `module::modbus::setup_dialog`, `setup` — setup dialog drops its read-range inputs; a device file's ranges and the running module's ranges survive confirm and `:edit` (MB-R-298, MB-R-299, MB-R-300)
- docs/specs/modbus — 15 entries added, 0 changed
- docs/specs/tui — `:module` command row, monitor-table note extended
- README — `:module` row in the command table, screenshot script reference fixed, screenshots regenerated

## Tests
15 of 15 entries pinned
- MB-R-295 — pinned by the module test of the rebuilt poll operations, plus the demo run's no-reconnect check

## Verification beyond the gauntlet
- user ran the demo TUI before the final review, on the tree before the live-error-box rework
  - `:module` opened on the client and server tabs, prefilled from the device's `read_ranges`
  - an invalid input showed the error border, and `Enter` was refused
  - `Esc`, then confirming the close popup, left the ranges unchanged
  - confirming new ranges on a running client kept polling without a reconnect (MB-R-295)
  - `:wd` saved the confirmed ranges
  - the error box refreshed only on confirm, which became the MB-R-301 live-box change
- user regenerated all screenshots with `images/capture.py`; the new-module dialog shows no range inputs
- not run: a demo re-check of the live error box after the rework, which `ut_error_box_tracks_inputs_live` pins

## Scenarios covered
All 6 scenarios of #398: S1 Change read ranges on a running client · S2 A malformed range is refused · S3 Leaving the dialog without saving · S4 Loading a module keeps the device file's ranges · S5 Editing a module keeps its ranges · S6 No module settings on a monitor tab

## Requirements
| ID | Issue | Kind | Requirement |
|---|---|---|---|
| MB-R-288 | R1 | new | `:module` on a Modbus client or server tab opens the module settings dialog, holding one single-line text input per `read_ranges` field: `holding`, `input`, `coils`, `discrete`. |
| MB-R-289 | R2 | new | Each input of the module settings dialog (MB-R-288) opens prefilled with the tab's current `read_ranges` value for its field, empty where that field is unset. |
| MB-R-290 | R3 | new | A Modbus monitor tab does not handle `:module`, so the application logs the unknown-command warning of UI-R-348. |
| MB-R-291 | R4 | new | A module settings dialog input (MB-R-288) whose text holds a malformed or reversed range entry paints the error border (UI-R-068) while that text stands. |
| MB-R-292 | R5 | new | `Enter` in the module settings dialog is refused while any input fails MB-R-291's check: the dialog stays open and the tab's `read_ranges` are unchanged. |
| MB-R-293 | R6 | new, reworded after filing | Confirming a module settings dialog whose inputs all parse sets the tab's in-memory device-config `read_ranges` to the inputs' trimmed text, a blank input leaving its field unset. |
| MB-R-294 | R7 | new, reworded after filing | Closing the module settings dialog through its close-confirmation popup (UI-R-023) leaves the tab's `read_ranges` unchanged, whether or not an input fails MB-R-291's check. |
| MB-R-295 | R8 | new | A running client polls with the `read_ranges` confirmed through the module settings dialog (MB-R-293, MB-R-083) from its next poll cycle on, without reconnecting. |
| MB-R-296 | R9 | new | Confirming the module settings dialog (MB-R-293) declares the gap cells of the confirmed `read_ranges` (MB-R-084) in the tab's store, a rejected declaration logged per MB-R-129. |
| MB-R-297 | R10 | new | Confirming the module settings dialog (MB-R-293) writes no file; `:write-device` saves the confirmed `read_ranges` into the device config. |
| MB-R-298 | R11 | new | The Modbus setup dialog, opened by the new-module flow, `:load`, `:edit` or `:clone`, carries no read-range input. |
| MB-R-299 | R12 | new | A tab created by confirming a Modbus setup dialog that names a device-config path takes that file's `read_ranges`. |
| MB-R-300 | R13 | new | Confirming `:edit`'s Modbus setup dialog leaves the tab's `read_ranges` unchanged. |
| MB-R-301 | R14 | new, reworded after filing | The module settings dialog's error message box tracks its inputs live: while any input fails MB-R-291's check it names the first failing input's field and its malformed entry, and it disappears once every input passes, as the add/edit register dialog shows its inline error (MB-R-224). |
| MB-E-105 | E1 | new | Gap cells declared for a read range that a later module settings dialog confirm removes (MB-R-296) stay declared in the store, so a read spanning them still succeeds. |

